{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And the children of Heth answered.</b> Rabbi Elazar said: How many reeds are broken and how much ink is spilled to write 'the children of Heth' ten times in the Torah — to teach you that whoever clarifies the portion of a righteous person, Scripture accounts it to him as if he fulfilled the five books of the Torah. And similarly we interpret in the Haggadah regarding ״ואיש בא מבעל שלישה״ and ״ויבא לאיש האלהים לחם בכורים״. And one asks: Was Elisha the priest one who eats first fruits? Rather, anyone who brings a gift to a Torah scholar — Scripture accounts it to him as if he built the Temple and brings first fruits. And likewise regarding the ban of Achan, חרם is written five times, also to say that whoever violates the ban is as if he transgressed the five books of the Torah. Another interpretation: Take the first letter and the last letter from each of the words at the end of the five books of the Torah, and they yield חרם: מ of במצרים, at the end of Genesis; ם of מסעיהם, at the end of Exodus; י of סיני, at the end of Leviticus; י of יריחו, at the end of Numbers; ם of ישראל, at the end of Deuteronomy — here is חרם."], :book :genesis, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The five letters מ-ם-י-י-ם are stated in order from Genesis through Deuteronomy; the compilers' letter-sequence produces מםיים, not חרם. The interpretation likely depends on variant readings of the final words of the books. Translated hyper-literally as the text stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:05:54.198946622Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}